Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 11:06:58 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 152/160] feat: the LMT slot knows what space a LUT is in,
 wraps a Rec.709 one, and hot reloads (S10 item 4)

Drop any ordinary look LUT into the Look slot and it works. Edit a cube on disk and the
picture follows. Pick both cube paths with a "..." button instead of typing them.

Research: scene-notes/research/s10-4-lmt-space-wrap.md (read first).
Decision: scene-notes/decisions/s10-grade-component.md section 4.

--- (a) the space tag -------------------------------------------------------------

A .cube may carry `# ReC-LUT-Space: <ACEScct|ACEScg|Rec709>`, which ParseCubeFile now
reads instead of skipping. When present it WINS: it was written by whoever made the LUT,
while the new `LMT Input Space` property is a guess by whoever loaded it. When absent,
the property decides. When both are present and disagree, the file wins and one log line
names the file, the tag and the property. An unrecognised tag is a warning and a
fall-through, not a refusal - a file that loads with a diagnosis beats one that does not.

Aliases, case-insensitive: ACEScct; ACEScg / Linear; Rec709 / Rec.709 / Rec709Display /
sRGB / sRGBDisplay / Display. Deliberately NOT accepted: ACES2065-1 and the camera log
spaces (LogC, SLog3, Log3G10) - naming them would promise a conversion we do not have.

The property's default is ACEScct, so an untagged LUT with nobody touching it takes
exactly the path it took before this commit: parse, convert, upload. Bit-identical, as a
consequence of the default rather than as a special case in the code.

Nothing is written back into the property - EntityObject.cpp:925 snapshots a component's
reflected members BEFORE :930 tells it they changed, so a member written from the handler
renders but does not reliably save (the evidence that killed two-way CDL binding in 3a).

--- (b) wrapping a foreign LUT ----------------------------------------------------

Most look LUTs in the world are made for a finished Rec.709 display picture. This chain
hands the LMT slot ACEScct. Both are three floats in [0,1] on a unit domain, so the wrong
one loads silently and the picture is wrong in a way that looks like a grade.

A LUT that is not ACEScct is now WRAPPED at load, on the CPU, onto a 65^3 grid, before
the fp16 conversion. The recipe is everybody's - the ACES guidance for reusing a legacy
show LUT, Baselight's and Resolve's inverse-ODT node sandwiches, Nuke's OCIODisplay
invert: forward output transform, the LUT, inverse output transform. For Rec.709 the two
halves are two cubes parsed but NEVER uploaded (odt_srgb_100nit_aces2_65 and its new
inverse), loaded lazily and only when something needs wrapping; for ACEScg it is the
analytic shaper, now available in C++.

What is ours is that it is applied as a RESIDUAL:

    W(x) = x + [ inv(LUT(fwd(x))) - inv(fwd(x)) ]

because inv(fwd(x)) is not the identity. An SDR output transform folds out-of-gamut
colour inwards and puts everything above display white on one code value, and neither can
be undone, so the plain sandwich turns an IDENTITY Rec.709 LUT into a visibly non-identity
LMT. In the residual form the two inv() calls land on the same coordinate for an identity
source and cancel, which keeps "a neutral is an exact identity" - the rule every other
control in this component obeys. No ACES document was found writing it this way, so it is
recorded as our variant with the argument, and the plain sandwich is kept as the
documented alternative (one constant).

MEASURED (tools/ocio-bake: uv run wrap.py --verify-identity), wrapping an IDENTITY Rec.709
LUT and comparing against the identity, over the whole grid, in ACEScct code values where
1.0 / 17.52 is one stop:

    grid  evaluator                       residual max   sandwich max
    33^3  plugin (two sampled cubes)      0.000000000    0.954338
    33^3  wrap.py (exact OCIO)            0.000000000    1.125571
    33^3  ACEScg (analytic shaper)        0.000000000    0.445205
    65^3  (all three)                     0.000000000    unchanged

The sandwich's 0.95-1.13 is 16-20 stops of error at the extremes; the residual's zero is
exact by construction, at any grid size and whatever the two ODT cubes' own error.

On real content - a third-party-style 709 look LUT (S-curve + warm/cool split + saturation
push), scoring ODT(W(x)) against LUT(ODT(x)) over colour inside the Rec.709 gamut and
inside the display's range:

    33^3 wrapped, plugin's evaluator   mean 0.96 cv8   p99 7.3   max 23.9
    65^3 wrapped, plugin's evaluator   mean 0.44 cv8   p99 3.5   max 15.5
    65^3 wrapped, exact OCIO           mean 0.34 cv8   p99 3.0   max 14.6

Two things follow, and both are in the code. The wrapped grid is ALWAYS 65^3 whatever the
source's was - a wrapped LUT carries the output transform's curvature as well as the
look's, and the ODT is much the steeper of the two, so the source's grid is the wrong
thing to key on. And the plugin's pair of sampled cubes costs only ~0.1 cv8 over OCIO's
exact processors, which is what justifies doing this at load at all.

The honest cost, documented: above display white the residual does NOT reproduce what the
LUT does to white. It carries the LUT's white-point shift up as a constant gain and lets
the ODT re-clip, instead of flattening every super-white value onto LUT(white). That
keeps a highlight separating on screen and keeps the highlight data in an EXR (the export
tap is downstream of the LMT). A print look that pulls white down still rolls toward its
lower white.

The output transform used for the wrap is FIXED at the sRGB SDR one. Which monitor a look
was authored on is a property of the LOOK, so pointing the camera at a different ODT must
not change what that look means.

The ODT slot never wraps: an output transform IS the step that leaves the grading space.

--- (c) hot reload ----------------------------------------------------------------

cinecam_LutHotReload <frames>, default 30 in the editor and 0 in the launcher, decided at
registration from gEnv->IsEditor() and overridable afterwards. Save over the cube the LMT
(or ODT) slot is using and the picture follows in about half a second.

A poll of (modification time, size) through ICryPak::GetModificationTime, on the SAME
OpenLutFile two-tree search the load used, so the watcher cannot end up watching a
different file from the one in the picture; a paked cube returns the archive entry's time
and never changes, which is correct.

Not IFileChangeMonitor, on evidence: Sandbox is the only provider in the whole tree
(EditorFileMonitor.cpp:30 is the only SetIFileChangeMonitor; the launcher's is null), and
even there MonitorDirectories() watches the project game folder, the mod paths, Editor/
and Engine/Shaders - NOT <engine>/engine/Assets/ODT/, which is one of the two install
trees this loader documents and logs absolute paths to disambiguate. A reload that works
in half the installs is worse than none.

Two safeties. A change must be seen TWICE before it is acted on, so a half-written file
(Resolve writes ~2.7 MB for a 65^3) is never loaded. And a failed RELOAD keeps the
previous good LUT and warns once with the reason - the user did not ask for a change, so
the picture must not change under them; a failed FIRST load still clears the slot, because
that one the user typed and needs to see (D13). One log line per successful reload.

--- (d) the shipped looks ---------------------------------------------------------

Documented in README.md and docs/SceneReferredLook.md; the cubes came in the previous
commit. No dropdown enum: work item 4b replaces the Look group with CryEngine asset
pickers and owns where the files live, so an enum built now would be undone.

--- (e) a "..." button on both cube paths -----------------------------------------

ODT File and LMT File become Schematyc::CineCubeFileName - the shipped
SResourceNameSerializer<&fn returning Serialization::ResourceFilePath> pattern
(ResourceTypes.h:255, Resources.h:60), the same family MannequinAnimationDatabasePath
belongs to, with a *.cube filter. The legacy yasli tree the entity inspector uses has the
row registered (PropertyRowResourceFilePath.cpp:99) and its button runs
CEngineFileDialog::RunGameOpen, which returns an ENGINE-RELATIVE path - exactly the string
the loader already resolves, so nothing about storage or existing levels changes (outside
the editor the type serialises as ar(*value.path, name, label), byte-for-byte what
CSharedString wrote).

Not the ASSET picker family (Schematyc::TextureFileName's): a .cube is not a registered
asset type in 5.7.1. That is item 4b.

Two stock quirks documented rather than patched: the row never copies the decorator's
startFolder (:53 copies filter/flags/handle only), so the start folder is inert; and the
dialog only offers files under the project, so a cube installed only into the engine tree
must still be typed - it still resolves and still loads.

